/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (NON-CUSTODIAL wallet \(trustless\) It gives users complete control over their private keys and cryptocurrencies/tokens. Users manage their own funds and are responsible for securing their private keys and executing transactions. Non-custodial wallets are favoured by those who prioritise self-sovereignty and trustless transactions, as well as those who want to engage with decentralised finance and applications \(DeFi and dApps\) and other Web3 technologies. In terms of security, non-custodial wallets, especially hardware wallets, are often considered the most secure option because they can be accessed and managed offline, reducing to almost zero the risk of to be hacked. However, the most important risk is a direct consequence of the fact that users must manage their own private keys; so, effectively being the owner of your money, in the event of loss of the private key, the result is the loss of funds and the impossibility of proving their possession.)
/E (NON-CUSTODIAL wallet \(trustless\) It gives users complete control over their private keys and cryptocurrencies/tokens. Users manage their own funds and are responsible for securing their private keys and executing transactions. Non-custodial wallets are favoured by those who prioritise self-sovereignty and trustless transactions, as well as those who want to engage with decentralised finance and applications \(DeFi and dApps\) and other Web3 technologies. In terms of security, non-custodial wallets, especially hardware wallets, are often considered the most secure option because they can be accessed and managed offline, reducing to almost zero the risk of to be hacked. However, the most important risk is a direct consequence of the fact that users must manage their own private keys; so, effectively being the owner of your money, in the event of loss of the private key, the result is the loss of funds and the impossibility of proving their possession.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
