/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (PERMISSIONLESS Public blockchains are open networks that allow anyone to join, interact, and participate in consensus validation without requiring any special permission. These blockchains are fully decentralised, meaning no single entity can control or shut down the network. Transactions on permissionless blockchains are transparent and can be viewed by anyone, but user identities are often anonymous or pseudonymous due to encryption methods. Examples of permissionless blockchains are Bitcoin and Ethereum. Even if the nature of blockchain is permissionless, it is still possible to work in a permissioned environment.)
/E (PERMISSIONLESS Public blockchains are open networks that allow anyone to join, interact, and participate in consensus validation without requiring any special permission. These blockchains are fully decentralised, meaning no single entity can control or shut down the network. Transactions on permissionless blockchains are transparent and can be viewed by anyone, but user identities are often anonymous or pseudonymous due to encryption methods. Examples of permissionless blockchains are Bitcoin and Ethereum. Even if the nature of blockchain is permissionless, it is still possible to work in a permissioned environment.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
