/T (PERMISSIONED Private blockchains are closed networks that require users to identify themselves and assign defined roles to perform only permitted activities. Permissioned blockchains are controlled by a central authority or a consortium of entities and can be partially decentralised or fully centralised. They offer enhanced privacy and security features, making them suitable for enterprise and government applications that require strict control over data and access. Examples of permissioned blockchains are: )
/E (PERMISSIONED Private blockchains are closed networks that require users to identify themselves and assign defined roles to perform only permitted activities. Permissioned blockchains are controlled by a central authority or a consortium of entities and can be partially decentralised or fully centralised. They offer enhanced privacy and security features, making them suitable for enterprise and government applications that require strict control over data and access. Examples of permissioned blockchains are: )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
