599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Central Bank Digital Currencies \(CBDCs\) and stablecoins are among the most significant innovations reshaping the digital monetary landscape. Each represents a distinct approach to digital finance and brings unique features with potential implications for the future of financial infrastructures, including those based on DLT.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (CBDCs are digital currencies issued and backed by national central banks. They are intended to provide the trust and stability of sovereign money while enabling faster, more secure, and programmable digital payments. However, the design of CBDCs varies considerably across jurisdictions, reflecting national policy priorities, technological capacities, and regulatory frameworks. These differences manifest in several key areas: whether the infrastructure is centralised or DLT-based, the extent of transaction programmability, privacy and data governance mechanisms and levels of regulatory oversight. These design choices have a direct impact on CBDCs' interoperability with blockchain systems and their integration into tokenised financial ecosystems.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Stablecoins, by contrast, are digital tokens issued by private entities and typically anchored to fiat currencies, most commonly at a 1:1 ratio. Built to operate on public blockchains, they are inherently interoperable, programmable and already serve a wide range of use cases in decentralised finance \(DeFi\), cross-border payments and tokenised asset markets. For certain issuers, stablecoins benefit from regulatory clarity, which, together with their technological readiness, makes them attractive for integration into existing financial systems.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
602 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
603 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Its architecture is still under development, with a planned launch around 2028, according to the most recent official updates. One of the central questions still under active discussion is its level of programmability, the ability to support automated transactions governed by smart contracts, which could play a critical role in enabling new financial instruments and settlement mechanisms on blockchain platforms.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
604 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (According to current ECB plans, it has been outlined a specific model for the distribution, which will occur through supervised intermediaries such as banks and payment service providers, which will also take care of the creation and the management of the digital wallets. At the same time, from a technological standpoint, the ECB is still exploring various options and architectures, including both centralised infrastructure and decentralised technologies like DLTs.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
