<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The discussion around the energy consumption of DLT and blockchain technology has reached unprecedented levels in recent years leading to questioning its sustainability and long-term viability in a world increasingly focused on reducing carbon footprints and combating climate change.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
957 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
958 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Traditional Alternative)
/Pg 42 0 R
>>
endobj
959 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Even if a direct comparison between blockchains and the energy consumption of financial sector activities may not be an appropriate comparison, the systems that blockchain aims to replace have a high energy footprint: traditional banking systems consume about 260 TWh annually, and, when factoring in data centres, electronic payment systems, and physical infrastructure \(bank branches, ATMs, etc.\), total consumption rises to around 700 TWh per year. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
960 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Value created)
/Pg 42 0 R
>>
endobj
961 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Regarding the value created, studies show that the adoption of blockchain reflects a direct and positive link with the improvement of environmental performance in terms of resource use and emission reduction, mainly due to the tracking, transparency and automation capabilities of the technology. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
962 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (One key potential application is the use of DLTs for improving data collection, sharing, and reporting, particularly concerning Environmental, Social, and Governance \(ESG\) factors: traditional approaches to ESG investing often face challenges related to consistent data, comparable metrics, and transparent methodologies, while DLTs, leveraging the potentially transparent and auditable nature of blockchains, could help to record ESG data. This could lead to more informed investment decisions, to address data availability challenges in ESG investing, to allow for the storage and distribution of large amounts of data related to companies' sustainability practices. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
endobj
963 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Furthermore, DLTs could potentially provide a single, auditable, verifiable, and traceable record of data collected across a company's supply chain, such as provenance data, greenhouse gas emissions tracking, and compliance information. This tamper-proof, near real-time tracking could offer increased transparency and accuracy in supply chain due diligence, potentially facilitating responsible supply chains and enabling better control and risk mitigation.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
964 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Another beneficial use case is the facilitation of tokenised sustainable finance products, such as green bonds: tokenised green bonds using DLTs have shown potential for increased transparency, making it easier for investors to track the positive environmental impact of projects in near real-time.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
965 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (However, some challenges remain in deploying DLTs for sustainable finance and ESG uses. A major issue is the quality and accuracy of data introduced onto the blockchain, known as the "garbage in, garbage out" problem: DLTs themselves cannot verify the trustworthiness of off-chain data. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [12 13]
>>
endobj
