/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 1848 0 R
/T (Latency refers to the time delay between initiating a transaction and it being confirmed on the blockchain. High latency usually reflects inefficient consensus or propagation mechanisms, which can lead to repeated communication attempts across nodes, increasing energy usage. On the other hand, low-latency systems reduce unnecessary back-and-forth communication across the network, lowering power consumption.)
/E (Latency refers to the time delay between initiating a transaction and it being confirmed on the blockchain. High latency usually reflects inefficient consensus or propagation mechanisms, which can lead to repeated communication attempts across nodes, increasing energy usage. On the other hand, low-latency systems reduce unnecessary back-and-forth communication across the network, lowering power consumption.)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
998 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 1849 0 R
/T (Throughput is the number of transactions a blockchain can process per second. A system with high throughput typically processes more transactions per block, which means less blocks are needed to clear the same volume of transactions, consuming less energy in the long run. This is especially relevant in capital markets, where batch processing of high transaction volumes is common \(e.g., bond issuance, clearing, and settlement\).)
/E (Throughput is the number of transactions a blockchain can process per second. A system with high throughput typically processes more transactions per block, which means less blocks are needed to clear the same volume of transactions, consuming less energy in the long run. This is especially relevant in capital markets, where batch processing of high transaction volumes is common \(e.g., bond issuance, clearing, and settlement\).)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
999 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 1850 0 R
/T (The design of nodes distribution, the number and geographic spread of nodes, affects communication costs and energy use. A highly decentralised network with thousands of globally distributed nodes may require longer data propagation paths and redundant validations, increasing energy usage. More nodes imply more redundancy leading to higher energy costs but improves security and resilience giving a higher level of decentralisation to the network. )
/E (The design of nodes distribution, the number and geographic spread of nodes, affects communication costs and energy use. A highly decentralised network with thousands of globally distributed nodes may require longer data propagation paths and redundant validations, increasing energy usage. More nodes imply more redundancy leading to higher energy costs but improves security and resilience giving a higher level of decentralisation to the network. )
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [34 35]
>>
